<h2>The shared household includes premises the family does not own</h2>
<p>Section 2(s) of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 defines the shared household to include a household where the aggrieved person lives or has lived with the respondent in a domestic relationship, whether owned or tenanted by either of them, and irrespective of whether either has any right, title or interest in it. A government quarter allotted to the husband, or a flat provided by his employer as a condition of service, therefore qualifies as a shared household for as long as the couple lives or has lived there together.</p>
<p>Section 17 then declares that every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household, whether or not she has any right, title or beneficial interest in it, and that she shall not be evicted or excluded from it except in accordance with the procedure established by law. On the face of the statute, the position of a wife in official accommodation is no weaker than that of a wife in a privately owned home.</p>

<h2>The service-licence problem</h2>
<p>The difficulty is not in the definition but in the nature of the premises. Official accommodation is held on a licence tied to service: the employee occupies it only while posted at that station, only while in service, and only while the allotment subsists. The licence typically ends on transfer, resignation, dismissal or retirement, or when the allotment is cancelled for breach of the allotment rules. Neither spouse ever acquires a tenancy or ownership that could outlast the service relationship.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h4>Privately owned matrimonial home</h4>
    <p>The respondent or his family holds title or tenancy. A residence order under Section 19 can realistically keep the aggrieved woman in the same premises for the long term, because the right she is protected in does not expire by efflux of the respondent's employment.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h4>Government or employer accommodation</h4>
    <p>Occupation rests on a service licence. The DV Act binds the respondent, not the government or the employer. When the allotment ends, the premises must be vacated, and the enduring relief shifts to alternate accommodation or rent under Section 19(1)(f).</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>This is the central point parties on both sides often miss. The DV Act creates rights <em>against the respondent</em>. It does not create any right against the allotting authority, which is ordinarily not even a party to the proceedings. A residence order can restrain the respondent from dispossessing the aggrieved woman or from surrendering the quarter to defeat her residence, but it cannot compel the government to continue an allotment that has lawfully ended, or to allot premises to a person who is not its employee.</p>

<h2>What happens when the allotment ends</h2>
<p>When the employee retires, is transferred, or the allotment is cancelled, government premises are recovered under a separate statutory track: the Public Premises (Eviction of Unauthorised Occupants) Act, 1971. Under that Act, the estate officer issues notice to the occupants, hears them, and may pass an eviction order against anyone in unauthorised occupation. Family members occupying the quarter after the licence has ended are generally treated as part of that unauthorised occupation. The pendency of a DV Act petition does not by itself convert occupation of public premises into authorised occupation.</p>
<div class="note"><p>An aggrieved woman living in official accommodation should treat the quarter as a time-limited refuge and build her case around Section 19(1)(f) from the outset, rather than discovering the limits of Section 17 only when the estate officer issues notice.</p></div>

<h2>Section 19(1)(f): the relief that survives</h2>
<p>Section 19 empowers the Magistrate, on being satisfied that domestic violence has taken place, to pass residence orders of several kinds. Clause (f) is the one designed for exactly this situation: the court may direct the respondent to secure the same level of alternate accommodation for the aggrieved person as enjoyed by her in the shared household, or to pay rent for such accommodation, if the circumstances so require.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Same level, not bare shelter</h4><p>The statutory benchmark is accommodation of the same level as the shared household. A wife who lived in a Type-V quarter in central Delhi cannot fairly be relocated to a single room on the periphery; parity of locality, size and amenity is the measure the court applies.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Rent as the alternative</h4><p>Where securing premises is impractical, the court can direct payment of rent. In practice this is quantified with reference to prevailing rents for comparable premises and is often combined with monetary relief under Section 20.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Operates against the respondent</h4><p>The direction binds the respondent personally. His retirement or transfer does not discharge it; if anything, the loss of official premises is precisely when the clause is meant to operate.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For the aggrieved woman:</strong> plead the quarter as the shared household under Section 2(s); seek protection against dispossession and against surrender of the allotment while it subsists; and claim, in the alternative, alternate accommodation or rent under Section 19(1)(f) with particulars of the standard of living enjoyed. Keep the allotment letter, licence-fee deductions in salary slips and proof of residence on record.</p>
  <p><strong>For the respondent:</strong> an order under Section 19 does not require him to act unlawfully towards his employer, but a voluntary surrender of the quarter made to defeat the residence order can invite consequences in the DV proceedings. The fair course is to place the allotment terms before the court and address the Section 19(1)(f) claim on the merits, including what level of alternate accommodation or rent is actually warranted.</p>
  <p><strong>For both sides:</strong> the Public Premises Act proceedings and the DV Act proceedings run on separate tracks before different authorities. Orders in one are not a substitute for defending the other.</p>
</div>
<p>The structure of the Act is coherent once the two tracks are kept apart. Section 17 protects possession as it exists; Section 19(1)(f) protects the standard of residence when that possession cannot continue. In official-accommodation cases, counsel who frame the petition around both from day one spare their client an avoidable crisis later.</p>
